<Step id="STEP_3_LOAD_RULES">
      <Goal>Internalize the rules governing this phase. No log line.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Read the rule files named on STEP_1's `READ rules:` line (`AX_RULES_LOAD_FROM_PHASE_BLOCK` +
        `AX_READ_PER_MANIFEST`) — the bounded set already filtered by phase kind + Target Files. A
        cited file that fails to resolve → `H_MISSING_RULE_FILE`. Skip the `rules` log line — the
        manifest already declares them.
      </Action>
    </Step>

<Axiom id="AX_READ_PER_MANIFEST">
      When a tool hands a read-manifest for the current step — the exact rule anchors, spec anchors,
      ticket sections, and Target Files to read — read EXACTLY that, nothing beyond it. The manifest's
      "do not read" list and line budget are binding.

      - Pull single blocks by anchor (`sdd-extract <file> <ANCHOR>`), not whole files: reading a fat file
        end-to-end for one section clogs context and degrades a cheaper model on long input.
      - Need something the manifest does not list? That signals the ticket or manifest is wrong — log a
        `discovery` line and surface it; never silently widen the read.
    </Axiom>

<Axiom id="AX_RULES_LOAD_FROM_PHASE_BLOCK">
      Open ONLY rule files listed under this phase's `Rules:` bullet list. Use the absolute-or-cwd-relative path verbatim. Read end-to-end (Mission + Belief_State + Reward_Criteria + Anti_Patterns + Verification_Hooks).

      One legal extension: when a cited rule file itself declares a transitive base — an explicit
      `InheritedBaseline` block, or text stating "read `<other-file>` first" — that declared base is
      part of the same rule for scoping purposes and MAY be opened too. It is NOT a discovery: log
      nothing (no `discovery` line), since the manifest's citation already covers it by way of the
      rule file's own declaration. This extension is narrow and self-limited by construction — it only
      ever reaches the base(s) the cited file names, never a sibling phase's rules, never a file the
      worker decides on its own looks relevant.

      Everything else stays ONLY: rules from sibling phases are NOT this phase's concern, do not
      pre-load them; a file that looks related but is not cited, and is not a declared base of a cited
      file, stays unread. Read failure on a cited rule (or its declared base) → `H_MISSING_RULE_FILE`.
    </Axiom>